{{also|bed-ridden}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|bed-ridden}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|bedredyn}}, {{m|enm|bedraden}}, {{m|enm|bedreden}}, {{m|enm|bæddrædæn}} (also as {{m|enm|bedreede}}, {{m|enm|bedrede}}), from {{inh|en|ang|bedreda|t=bedridden|pos=adj.}}, from {{m|ang|bedreda}}, {{m|ang|bedrida}}, {{m|ang|bæddryda|t=one who is bedridden|pos=noun}}, from {{m|ang|bed}}, {{m|ang|bedd|t=bed}} + {{m|ang|rida||rider}}, with {{m|en|-en}} by analogy with past participle adjectives.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈbɛdɹɪdən/}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# [[confined|Confined]] to [[bed]] because of [[infirmity]] or [[illness]].
#* {{RQ:Macaulay History of England|chapter=24|passage=the estate of a '''bedridden''' old gentleman}}

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|confined to bed}} {{l|en|bedfast}}, {{l|en|bedbound}}

====Derived terms====
{{der3|en|bedriddenness}}

